# `examples-rebel/PACKAGES.md`, Rebel's packages indexed for a model that
# writes a program with them: how to take Rebel and how to read the index,
# the faces by layer each with its purpose, then each face's declarations on
# one line each, every word read from what `heroes doc` prints, so a name the
# index gives is one the face declares and a call it shows is one the checker
# accepts.

use faces
use prose
use reference

# The site whose reference pages are `heroes doc`'s output, one per face
# (`site/src/lib/reference.ts`, `referenceUrl`).
constant SITE: str
    "https://heroes-lang.org"

# The address of the reference page of `face`.
#
# - `face`: the face's path, `unicode/segment`.
function page_of(face: str) -> str
    return SITE + "/rebel/" + face + "/"

# How many declarations of each kind the references hold.
record Counted
    # Functions.
    functions: i64
    # Records.
    records: i64
    # Variants.
    variants: i64
    # Constants.
    constants: i64

# The whole of `PACKAGES.md`.
#
# - `version`: Rebel's version, the folder the faces were read from.
# - `rows`: README § A1's rows, each with its faces.
# - `references`: each face's reference, by its path.
# - `vocabulary`: README § A4's codes, in the table's order.
#
# Fails `not_found` where a face of `rows` has no reference.
function packages_page(version: str, rows: [faces.Row], references: {str: reference.Reference}, vocabulary: {str: bool}) -> str?
    counted @= Counted(functions: 0, records: 0, variants: 0, constants: 0)
    listed: [str] @= []
    sections: [str] @= []
    face_count @= 0
    layers = layers_of(rows)

    for layer in layers
        listed @ listed.push(f"### Layer {layer}\n")

        for row in rows
            if row.layer != layer
                continue

            for face in row.faces
                r = references[face]?
                face_count @ face_count + 1
                listed @ listed.push(f"- [`{face}`]({page_of(face)}): " + prose.lead(r.doc))
                sections @ sections.push(face_section(face: face, layer: layer, r: r, vocabulary: vocabulary))

                for d in r.declarations
                    counted @ tally(counted: counted, kind: d.kind)

        listed @ listed.push("")

    codes: [str] @= []

    for code in keys(vocabulary)
        codes @ codes.push(prose.code(code))

    head = [
        f"# Rebel {version}: every package, for a program that uses it"
        ""
        f"Rebel is the library a Heroes program takes or leaves, never the language's own. This index names every face of its version {version}, the modules a program names with `use <rebel>/<face>`: {prose.counted(count: face_count, one: "face", many: "faces")} in {prose.counted(count: layers.len(), one: "layer", many: "layers")}, with every one of their {prose.counted(count: counted.functions, one: "function", many: "functions")}, {prose.counted(count: counted.records, one: "record", many: "records")}, {prose.counted(count: counted.variants, one: "variant", many: "variants")} and {prose.counted(count: counted.constants, one: "constant", many: "constants")}. Every word of it is read from what `heroes doc` prints of each face, so a name it gives is one the face declares, and a call it shows is one the checker accepts: write no name it does not give."
        ""
        "## How to take Rebel"
        ""
        "A program names a package of Rebel through its catalogue, `heroes.toml`, which stands beside the file it compiles and which `heroes add` writes, run in the program's folder: nobody types a catalogue. From an index that publishes Rebel:"
        ""
        "    heroes add rebel --index <address>"
        "    heroes add rebel/csv"
        ""
        "or from a checkout of the repository, Rebel read where it stands:"
        ""
        f"    heroes add --path <checkout>/packages/rebel/{version}/files --as rebel"
        "    heroes add rebel/csv"
        ""
        "`heroes add rebel/<face>` adds a face to the entry's `packages` line, which names every face the program uses: a `use` of a face the line does not name is refused, and an entry fetched from an index places the faces it names and the modules they use, nothing else. A module then writes `use <rebel>/csv` and calls the face by the last part of its path: `csv.parse(text)`."
        ""
        "## How to read this index"
        ""
        "- The faces stand by layer, from 0: a package uses only packages of a lower layer (`examples-rebel/README.md` § A1). The list below names each with its purpose, the first sentence of its module comment; then each face has a section of its own, headed `### <face>`, in the same order."
        "- A section opens with the face's layer, the `use` line that names it, and its reference page, which shows every declaration whole: each parameter, its edges and its examples. `heroes doc <rebel>/<face>`, run in a program's folder, prints the same text."
        "- Then each declaration of the face, in the order the face writes it, on one line:"
        "  - a function: its signature as `heroes doc` prints it, the call a program writes (each argument its parameter's name, with a label exactly where the call needs one, `@` where the function changes the caller's place), the first sentence of its comment, and the codes its comment names from its `Fails` on, or, where it names none, its `Fails` sentence whole, a failure handed on from what it calls;"
        "  - a record: its fields in their declared order, written as the call that builds one, `record Name(field: type, ...)`; where a field's type is a private module's (`stream.State`), only the face's functions build it, as its comment says;"
        "  - a variant: its cases as a program writes them, `variant Name = .case | .case(field: type)`;"
        "  - a constant: its declaration, then its value where it is written on one line; a failure code is one of them."
        "- A type another face declares is written qualified, `io.Stream`, as the face writes it."
        "- A failure's code is one word of a closed vocabulary, the same in every package (`examples-rebel/README.md` § A4): " + codes.join(", ") + "."
        ""
        "## The faces by layer"
        ""
    ]
    tail = [
        "---"
        ""
        f"This index is written by `generators/rebel_index/`, never by hand, from `heroes doc`'s reference of every face of `packages/rebel/{version}/files/` and from `examples-rebel/README.md` § A1 and § A4. Change those, then run `heroes run generators/rebel_index/main.hero -- write ./heroes` from the repository's root; `check` in place of `write` says whether this index is still what they make."
    ]
    return ok(
        head.join("\n") + "\n" + listed.join("\n") + "## Each face\n\n" + sections.join("\n") + tail.join("\n") + "\n"
    )

# The layers of `rows`, each once, from the lowest.
#
# - `rows`: README § A1's rows.
function layers_of(rows: [faces.Row]) -> [i64]
    seen: {i64: bool} @= {}

    for row in rows
        seen[row.layer] @ true

    return sort(keys(seen))

# `counted` with one more declaration of the kind `kind`.
#
# - `counted`: the counts so far.
# - `kind`: `function`, `record`, `variant` or `constant`.
function tally(counted: Counted, kind: str) -> Counted
    return Counted(
        functions: counted.functions + one_if(kind == "function"),
        records: counted.records + one_if(kind == "record"),
        variants: counted.variants + one_if(kind == "variant"),
        constants: counted.constants + one_if(kind == "constant")
    )

# 1 where `yes`, else 0.
#
# - `yes`: the condition.
function one_if(yes: bool) -> i64
    if yes
        return 1
    return 0

# The section of `face`: its head, then each declaration on its line.
#
# - `face`: the face's path.
# - `layer`: its row's layer.
# - `r`: its reference.
# - `vocabulary`: README § A4's codes.
function face_section(face: str, layer: i64, r: reference.Reference, vocabulary: {str: bool}) -> str
    lines: [str] @= [
        "### " + face
        ""
        f"Layer {layer}, named `use <rebel>/{face}`, [its reference]({page_of(face)})."
        ""
        prose.lead(r.doc)
        ""
    ]

    for d in r.declarations
        lines @ lines.push(declaration_line(d: d, vocabulary: vocabulary))

    return lines.join("\n") + "\n\n"

# One declaration on one line: its signature, a function's call, its first
# sentence, and the codes a function's `Fails` paragraphs name.
#
# - `d`: the declaration.
# - `vocabulary`: README § A4's codes.
function declaration_line(d: reference.Declaration, vocabulary: {str: bool}) -> str
    out @= "- " + prose.code(reference.signature(d))
    shown = reference.value(d)

    if d.kind == "function"
        out @ out + ", called " + prose.code(d.call)
    else if shown.len() > 0
        out @ out + ", value " + prose.code(shown)
    sentence = prose.lead(d.doc)

    if sentence.len() > 0
        out @ out + ": " + sentence
    codes: [str] @= []

    if d.kind == "function"
        for code in reference.codes_named(doc: d.doc, vocabulary: vocabulary)
            codes @ codes.push(prose.code(code))

    if codes.len() > 0
        out @ out + " Fails " + codes.join(", ") + "."
    else if d.kind == "function"
        handed = reference.fails_sentence(d.doc)

        if handed.len() > 0
            out @ out + " " + handed
    return out

## Tests

test "page_of: a face's reference page, a nested one too"
    assert page_of("csv") == "https://heroes-lang.org/rebel/csv/"
    assert page_of("unicode/segment") == "https://heroes-lang.org/rebel/unicode/segment/"

test "declaration_line: a function with its call and codes, a constant with its value, a record"
    vocabulary = {"invalid": true}
    f = reference.Declaration(
        kind: "function",
        name: "parse",
        block: ["function parse(text: str) -> [[str]]?"],
        doc: ["Every row of `text`.", "", "Fails `invalid` where it is not CSV."],
        call: "csv.parse(text)"
    )
    assert declaration_line(d: f, vocabulary: vocabulary) == "- `function parse(text: str) -> [[str]]?`, called `csv.parse(text)`: Every row of `text`. Fails `invalid`."
    c = reference.Declaration(
        kind: "constant",
        name: "INVALID",
        block: ["constant INVALID: str", "    \"invalid\""],
        doc: ["The code."],
        call: ""
    )
    assert declaration_line(d: c, vocabulary: vocabulary) == "- `constant INVALID: str`, value `\"invalid\"`: The code."
    r = reference.Declaration(
        kind: "record",
        name: "P",
        block: ["record P", "    # X.", "    x: i64"],
        doc: [],
        call: ""
    )
    assert declaration_line(d: r, vocabulary: vocabulary) == "- `record P(x: i64)`"

test "layers_of: each layer once, from the lowest"
    rows = [
        faces.Row(layer: 2, package: "b", faces: ["b"])
        faces.Row(layer: 0, package: "a", faces: ["a"])
        faces.Row(layer: 2, package: "c", faces: ["c"])
    ]
    assert layers_of(rows) == [0, 2]
